  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 156 PM CDT Wed Sep 9 2026\n\nAfter several days of heat, relief has arrived. A cold front has \nmoved across the region with some noticeably cooler air behind it. \nHighs today will top out in the 70s and 80s, making it a fairly \npleasant day. A few models still leave low end PoPs along the \nKS/NE border this afternoon, but most areas are expected to stay\ndry. Still a few clouds across portions of southeastern \nNebraska and southwestern Iowa this afternoon, with additional \nclouds moving into northern Nebraska expected tonight. Light \nnorth winds, clearing skies, radiational cooling, and recent \nrainfall may aid in some patchy fog development early tomorrow \nmorning, particularly for areas along the Missouri River. There \nis a little less certainty in fog potential in extreme \nsoutheastern Nebraska/southwestern Iowa, where cloud cover is \nexpected to hang on a little later into the overnight hours. \n\nThursday, and particularly Friday, bring a warm up as a mid-level \nridge sets up over the Big Horns (WY) and gradually makes its way \nacross the Dakotas and Nebraska. Expect low 80s for Thursday highs \nand low to mid-80s on Friday. A cold front will drop into the region \non Friday, bringing chances for showers and thunderstorms. \nInstability and daytime heating will be factors as the front and a \nmid-level shortwave trough arrive. Strong to severe thunderstorms \nare expected Friday along with a possible concern for some flooding.\n\nCooler weather is expected heading into the weekend, particularly \nfor the northern half of the CWA, with highs in the 70s. Lows will \nbe in the 50s and 60s each morning. Periodic showers and \nthunderstorms will be possible through the course of the\nweekend.",
